dataset(wages).glm(union ~ educ)
---
error[Z0406]: `glm` needs `family:`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:16
  |
1 | dataset(wages).glm(union ~ educ)
  |                ^^^ `family:` is missing
  |
  = help: a binary outcome is `family: binomial`; a linear model is `reg`

====
frame(y = [0, 1, 2, 1], x = [1, 2, 3, 4]).glm(y ~ x, family: binomial)
---
error[Z0401]: `glm` with `family: binomial` needs an outcome of 0 and 1, or true and false, but `y` holds 2
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:47
  |
1 | frame(y = [0, 1, 2, 1], x = [1, 2, 3, 4]).glm(y ~ x, family: binomial)
  |                                               ^^^^^ not a binary outcome
  |
  = help: a yes-or-no outcome is coded 0 and 1 or as a Bool: `add(y_yes = y == ...)`; a numeric outcome is a linear model, `reg`

====
dataset(wages).glm(educ ~ exper, family: binomial)
---
error[Z0401]: `glm` with `family: binomial` needs an outcome of 0 and 1, or true and false, but `educ` holds 17
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:20
  |
1 | dataset(wages).glm(educ ~ exper, family: binomial)
  |                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^ not a binary outcome
  |
  = help: a yes-or-no outcome is coded 0 and 1 or as a Bool: `add(educ_yes = educ == ...)`; a numeric outcome is a linear model, `reg`

====
dataset(wages).glm(industry ~ educ, family: binomial)
---
error[Z0401]: the outcome `industry` is a category, and `glm` with `family: binomial` needs 0 and 1, or true and false
 --> scripts/main.zeff:1:20
  |
1 | dataset(wages).glm(industry ~ educ, family: binomial)
  |                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a category on the left of `~`
  |
  = help: say which level is the 1: `add(industry_1 = industry == "Public administration")` and fit `industry_1`

